A||||15||dravenhawk||clutch pedal will not depress-car cant be started||||1||
Z||000000||dravenhawk||02-05-2000||02:00 AM||||84 2.5<P>just purchased my first fiero. the clutch pedal will not depress. does anyone know if it is more likely the master or slave cylinder? or how to tell before purchasing the incorrect cylinder? or maybe it is something else?<P>thanks dravenhawk<p>[This message has been edited by dravenhawk (edited 02-05-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phaeton||02-05-2000||03:08 AM||||Silly question time.  The clutch will not physically depress?  Pedal and arm are up and locked?  Clutch pedal physically goes down but clutch does not disengage?  Pedal goes down but switch does not enable starter solenoid?  This is depressing.  If the pedal actually won't move then whatever is binding it in the footwell area needs a hammer and pliers taken to it. There is a neutral start switch that must be depressed also or the key will not get power to the starter.  A book should be available at a local parts store that costs less than most parts, it will have pictures and troubleshooting guides.  Daisy Duke is not in the pictures, but get it anyway.||||reg||1
Z||000002||dravenhawk||02-05-2000||03:51 AM||||the pedal cannot be depressed.<BR>i was told that the aluminum clutch master cylinder can seize up after the car has not been cranked in several months, and this would keep the clutch pedal from depressing.<P>i have the haynes book, however it does not address this issue.<P>dravenhawk<P>ps- any info on the 84 models catching on fire and what needs to be done to prevent this?||||reg||1
Z||000003||WKDFIRO||02-05-2000||04:11 AM||||For 84s it would help to be a fanatic when it comes to your oil levels. As I recall for whatever reason a pushrod would decide to leave it home in the block and spew oil on the exhaust manifold. Next thing you know you are on the side of the road proving the stereotype of Fieros being firebombs. Welcome to the Fiero Forum!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phaeton||02-05-2000||04:21 AM||||The master cyl rod is held to the pedal by a pin and keeper, fairly easy to get off.  It has to come off to replace master cylinder, so might as welltake it off first and amke sure pedal travels freely.  If pedal works then go downline and check for mechanical binding on master or slave rods and on lever assembly.  THEN you can replace master cylinder, although I recall that in a similar situation a certain member recommended more dialkyldithiophosphate as the solution. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phaeton||02-05-2000||04:47 AM||||I must be getting sick 'cause I can't sleep, heck I'm not even tired. Dang but this is going to catch up later.<BR>Meanwhile the odds are actually good that something other than the master cylinder is seizing.  Lots and lots of lube in the cylinder so it should not freeze up from nonuse unless major contamination is present, bad enough that a visual check would show it.  Spoon some into a glass, it should be clear or translucent, dirty is expected but should be uniform, no floaters, odd colors or zones.<BR>The shaft hooked to the lever connected to the throwout bearing does not live in an oil bath plus the loading is applied off center, maybe it was cleaned and lubed last clutch change?  Is the slave rod in a straight line with the cylinder?  <BR>Pages and pages have been devoted to Fiero clutch systems.  If something straightforward doesn't show up, keep smiling and enjoy the learning process.||||reg||1
Z||000006||kidzwheelz||02-05-2000||11:03 AM||||  I just got my 84 SE running last week. It was about a one year project to get it where I am now. (I work two jobs, and one of them is for uncle Sam i.e. U.S. Air Force) Anyway, to get to the point, I had to replace the slave cylinder because the entire guts had corroded solid, but I could still move the pedal! My guess is that your problem is further back up the line, probably in the master cylinder or pedal linkage.||||reg||4
Z||000007||dravenhawk||02-05-2000||12:43 PM||||thanks for the advice. the car has a new clutch so i guess i'll get started.<BR>what is dialkyldithiophosphate?<P>once again thanks<BR>dravenhawk<P>ps - where is the starter safety switch located? i would like to press it down and try and start the car.||||reg||6
Z||000008||Carrolles||02-05-2000||12:57 PM||||The starter safety switch is under the dash at the pedal.  It is mounted to a bracket and has a sort of plunger that is attached to the clutch pedal.  It will have a connector attached with two very heavy wires.  I believe they will be yellow or orange or both, I'm not sure.<P>You can unplug it and short out the contacts to get continuity to start.<P>My 85 se v-6 4spd is missing this switch.  The wires are simply shorted together.<P>Hope this helps,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||02-05-2000||01:11 PM||||crack open the bleed screw on the slave. If the slave is jammed then you'll know in about 15 second after you crack the bleeder. ((That's how long it will probly take you to reach down and push on the pedal.)) Either the pedal will drop like a rock or it still won't move. problem solved.||||reg||1
Z||000010||dravenhawk||02-05-2000||01:53 PM||||ogre - if the pedal still wont move are you saying that my problem is solved because i know it is the master, not the slave? i am very new to fieros. i have been a mustang 5.0 guy for 14 years.<P>carrolles - thanks for the tip on the switch<P>dravenhawk||||reg||5
Z||000011||Phaeton||02-05-2000||02:25 PM||||Problem solved?  What a cruel jest, you are a Fiero owner, your problems are just beginning.  Looks to be your first guess was the right one (maybe).  If none of the hard parts are hung up and the master won't push fluid down an open line...<BR>Dialkyldithiophosphate is what Formula was drinking last night, it also removes stains and enhances sex appeal (I hope I didn't slick my hair down with it for nothing).||||reg||14
Z||000012||theogre||02-05-2000||02:29 PM||||Yup, that's what I'm saying. It's how you check any siezed hydro clutch, not just fiero. It doesn't matter if air gets in since you have to take it apart to fix either one and then bleed anyway. just make sure fluid can't spray on anything if the pedal moves.<P>Before you even do start the car change the oil. god knows how long it's been. drive it a week and change it agian. 84's are very crabby about dirty oil. ||||reg||1
Z||000013||dravenhawk||02-05-2000||10:06 PM||||what a great forum. you guys are great for helping a newbie fiero owner.<P>dravenhawk||||reg||1
Z||000014||Oreif||02-05-2000||10:34 PM||||As for Fiero fires the 2 things that have been know to cause fires are: 1) The thermostat housing was originally a 2 piece set-up with a weld. This had a tendency to crack and drip antifreeze onto the exhaust. The cooking antifreeze fumes can catch fire. The original recall for this was to replace the housing with a one piece (no welds) one. and 2) GM got cheap in the '80s with the valve covers for the 2.5L engines. They didn't use a real gasket just silicone/rubber simular to form-a-gasket stuff. With time this would develop leaks and also end up on exhaust manifolds. Oil is more flamable than antifreeze. Most auto parts stores do list a cork gasket for a 2.5L. Also note this problem was with ALL 2.5L engines. Not just the Fiero. There is a tech bulletin and GM dealerships that if a 2.5 comes in for a valve cover oil leak to clean cover and install a gasket! The only reason the Fiero got such a bad rap was it's first year and the "pun" of the name.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 02-05-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||Standard||02-07-2000||02:25 AM||||1 in 4 Fiero's in 84 also had bad connecting rods, that were supplied by a plant in Saginow(sp?).  They tended to give at high rpms, punch a hole somewhere and spew oil on hot exhaust manifold.  Another problem was that the 84's originally only used 3 quarts of oil, which isn't much.. used a larger oil filter to up capacity to 4 quarts in the recalls.. According to Pontiac every single Fiero made in 84 has had the recall work done.  ||||reg||1
